merge("entities/donald-trump.md", u"""
## June 2024 items

Badlands commentary in June 2024 highlighted a December 2021 interview Trump gave to Israel's Channel 12, characterized as state media, in which he ran what [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] described as a psychological warfare campaign against Prime Minister [[benjamin-netanyahu|Benjamin Netanyahu]].[{N}] Trump recounted cutting Netanyahu off mid-sentence to say "Bibi, you don't want to make a deal," promoted [[benny-gantz|Benny Gantz]] and [[mahmoud-abbas|Mahmoud Abbas]] as the region's two genuine peacemakers, and stated "I don't think Bibi ever wanted to make peace; never did."[{N}] Of Abbas he said "I thought he wanted to make a deal more than Netanyahu... he was almost like a father," and concluded "I always thought that the Palestinians were impossible, and that the Israelis would do anything to make a deal... I found that not to be true."[{N}] The commentary observed that while some of these remarks had surfaced in memoirs reported by obscure outlets, the Channel 12 appearance itself went largely undiscussed, and that Trump said he was polling above 80% among Israeli citizens at the time — offered as partial explanation for civil unrest in Israel against Netanyahu.[{N}]

Trump's persona was also in the news through Ramin Setoodeh's book Apprentice in Wonderland, which reported that Trump kept the [[the-apprentice|Apprentice]] set intact on the 14th floor of Trump Tower for at least six months after launching his 2015 campaign in case the run failed.[{N}]

On June 6, 2024, a week after being found guilty on 34 counts in New York, Trump announced at a Turning Point Action town hall in Arizona that his campaign had raised roughly $400 million since the verdict, an unprecedented sum unlikely to be matched by [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] before the election.[{N}] He described the proceeding as "a rigged trial in New York with a highly conflicted... judge where there was no crime," argued that "they could have brought the case seven years ago" and that "it's only when you run for office they bring cases," and claimed he was beating Biden "by a lot."[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/benjamin-netanyahu.md", u"""
## Gantz withdrawal and Trump's Channel 12 remarks

On June 9, 2024, [[benny-gantz|Benny Gantz]] withdrew his National Unity party from Netanyahu's emergency wartime government, accusing the prime minister of preventing Israel from achieving "true victory" over [[hamas|Hamas]] and calling for elections, after Netanyahu failed to accept an agreed-upon vision for the Gaza conflict by a June 8 deadline Gantz had set.[{N}] Badlands commentary noted that Gantz had been widely seen as the moderate in the war cabinet and that his exit would leave Netanyahu surrounded by Zionist-maximalists in his security cabinet.[{N}]

[[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] argued that [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]] had waged a propaganda campaign against Netanyahu in December 2021 through Israel's Channel 12, quoting Trump as saying he told Netanyahu "you don't want to make a deal," that "I don't think Bibi ever wanted to make peace; never did," and that Netanyahu merely "tapped us along."[{N}] Trump was said to have contrasted Netanyahu unfavorably with Gantz and [[mahmoud-abbas|Mahmoud Abbas]], and to have been polling above 80% with Israeli citizens at the time, which the commentary linked to subsequent Israeli civil unrest against Netanyahu.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/mahmoud-abbas.md", u"""
## Trump's characterization and the alleged kayfabe

In June 2024 [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] published a video arguing that [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]] and Abbas have engaged in a form of kayfabe — staged public antagonism concealing cooperation — dating from before the Saudi "Sword Dance" summit of May 20, 2017.[{N}] In support he cited a December 2021 Israeli Channel 12 interview in which Trump said of Abbas, "I thought he wanted to make a deal more than Netanyahu. I had a great meeting with him... He was almost like a father," and named Abbas and [[benny-gantz|Benny Gantz]] as the two figures capable of making peace.[{N}] Trump added that his prior assumption that "the Palestinians were impossible, and that the Israelis would do anything to make a deal" proved false.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/vladimir-putin.md", u"""
## Dismissal of NATO invasion claims (June 2024)

At a meeting with the heads of major international news agencies on the sidelines of the [[2024-st-petersburg-international-economic-forum|St. Petersburg International Economic Forum]] in June 2024, Putin was asked about [[nato|NATO]]'s preparations to defend against a Russian invasion and dismissed the premise in blunt terms: "Look, someone has imagined that Russia wants to attack NATO. Have you gone completely insane? Are you as thick as a plank? Who came up with this nonsense, this bulls**t?"[{N}] He characterized the idea as a stupid attempt to maintain the West's global hegemony through fear, and suggested the claim served to deceive Western publics into arming themselves and sending more weapons to Ukraine.[{N}]

[[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] read the statement as an instance of Russian [[maneuver-defense|maneuver defense]] applied to information warfare, and placed it alongside Russia's naval deployment to the Caribbean as part of a wider pattern of moves and countermoves.[{N}] He also noted that [[el-salvador|El Salvador]]'s [[nayib-bukele|Nayib Bukele]] gets along well with Putin.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/nato.md", u"""
## Putin's June 2024 rejection of invasion claims

Asked at the [[2024-st-petersburg-international-economic-forum|St. Petersburg International Economic Forum]] in June 2024 about NATO's preparations to defend against a Russian invasion, [[vladimir-putin|Vladimir Putin]] dismissed the idea as "bulls**t" and as a fear-based attempt to preserve Western global hegemony, arguing it served to persuade Western publics to arm and to send more weapons to Ukraine.[{N}]

Badlands commentary framed the exchange as [[maneuver-defense|maneuver defense]] in the information domain, noting that while the Germans invented maneuver warfare, the Russians became its masters, and closing with the warning: "Keep playing games, NATO. The Sovereign Alliance is playing for keeps."[{N}]
""")

merge("concepts/maneuver-defense.md", u"""
## Application to Putin's 2024 NATO remarks

In June 2024 [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] applied the concept to [[vladimir-putin|Vladimir Putin]]'s dismissal of claims that Russia planned to attack [[nato|NATO]], describing the rhetorical posture as Russian maneuver defense.[{N}] He recounted the doctrine's origins as the Soviet answer to the German Blitzkrieg: feign retreat, draw advancing tanks deep so that a "head" forms well past the defensive lines, then use flanking positions the Russians called the "shoulders" to envelop the point of penetration — the "neck" — and decapitate the attacking force so it can be encircled and destroyed.[{N}] The technique was known as "Shoulder Defense," and the commentary claims it was so successful that Hitler denied ever using the term "Blitzkrieg," calling it a silly word.[{N}] The conclusion drawn was that although the Germans invented maneuver warfare, the Russians became its masters.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/hunter-biden.md", u"""
## Overlap between family business and presidential aides (June 2024 reporting)

Politico reporting in June 2024 found that for years [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] shared a bookkeeper with Hunter, and that when [[jim-biden|Jim Biden]] wanted to learn more about one of Hunter's associates he hired the former head of Joe Biden's Secret Service detail to investigate.[{N}] House impeachment inquiry interviews, public records and emails reviewed by the outlet showed that many of the president's closest staffers and advisers doubled as his relatives' business associates, both during and after their government service, complicating efforts to distance the president from the family's ventures.[{N}] The report noted that several of Jim and Hunter Biden's business partners had been convicted of federal fraud and corruption crimes in recent years.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/jim-biden.md", u"""
## Politico reporting on shared advisers (June 2024)

A June 2024 Politico investigation reported that [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] shared a personal lawyer with his brother Jim, and that when Jim wanted to know more about one of [[hunter-biden|Hunter Biden]]'s associates he hired the former head of Joe Biden's Secret Service detail to investigate him.[{N}] The reporting described an all-in-the-family approach to business and politics dating back half a century to Joe Biden's first Senate bid, which was run primarily by his parents and siblings, with political patrons at times forging business ties with relatives who then converted business partners into campaign supporters.[{N}] Jim and Hunter Biden's foreign dealings, and the federal fraud and corruption convictions of several of their business partners, brought renewed scrutiny to any potential links to the president.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/juan-merchan.md", u"""
## Juror comment disclosure (June 2024)

In early June 2024 Merchan provided the lawyers in the [[trump-hush-money-case|Trump hush money case]] with information about a comment posted on the court's Facebook page reading, "My cousin is a juror and says Trump is getting convicted. Thank you folks for all your hard work!!!" — a remark indicating that a juror may have disclosed a predetermined guilty verdict.[{N}] Conservative Treehouse called the public release "very sketchy," arguing that Merchan "doesn't have an integrity bone" and that the comment he chose to highlight was innocuous and random.[{N}] The outlet speculated that either something bigger was being diluted by the story, or Merchan was provoking [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]] into discussing jurors and thereby violating his gag order ahead of sentencing, or he was seeking to create a mistrial in order to exit the case or restart it with an extended gag order.[{N}]
""")

merge("concepts/trump-hush-money-case.md", u"""
## Aftermath of the verdict (June 2024)

On May 30, 2024, [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]] was found guilty on 34 counts of falsifying records, a charge Modernity News characterized as something "no one really quite knows."[{N}] A week later, on June 6, Trump announced at a Turning Point Action town hall in Arizona that his campaign had raised roughly $400 million since the verdict, an unprecedented amount that commentators judged unlikely to be matched by [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] before the election.[{N}] Trump said he had "just went through a rigged trial in New York with a highly conflicted, and I mean highly conflicted, judge where there was no crime," and argued that prosecutors "could have brought the case seven years ago" but that "it's only when you run for office they bring cases."[{N}]

In the same period Judge [[juan-merchan|Juan Merchan]] disclosed to counsel a Facebook comment on the court's page claiming that a juror's cousin had said Trump was going to be convicted, prompting speculation that Merchan was either diluting a larger story, baiting Trump into a gag order violation, or laying groundwork for a mistrial.[{N}]
""")

